  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A trough of low pressure will move\neastward across Northern Nevada this afternoon and this evening,\nresulting in a slight chance of valley rain and mountain snow. \nAny precipitation associated with this atmospheric disturbance \nwill remain north of Interstate 80. Precipitation chances will be \nhighest near the Nevada/Idaho border. Precipitation amounts will \nbe no higher than one tenth of an inch. Southwest wind gusts near\n40 mph are expected in Northern Nevada this afternoon into this\nevening. Blowing dust will reduce visibilities at times. Travel\nwill be difficult, particularly for high profile vehicles. \n\nZonal flow aloft is anticipated Tuesday through Thursday. This\nwill result in dry weather and temperatures slightly below\nseasonal values. An Eastern Pacific storm system may begin to\nimpact Northern and Central Nevada as early as Friday morning.\nPrecipitation chances will increase this weekend as this storm\nsystem passes through the Silver State. Snow levels could drop to\nthe valley floor Saturday night. Here are probabilities of one \ntenth of an inch of snow or more for select locations in Northern\nand Central Nevada from 5 PM PDT Saturday, October 31st, 2026 \nuntil 4 PM PDT Sunday, November 1st, 2026: \n\nAustin - 50%\nEureka - 25% \nEly - 20%\nLamoille - 20% \nOwyhee - 20% \nSpring Creek - 15% \nTonopah - 10% \nWells - 10%\nElko - 5%\nCarlin - 5%\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding strong wind gusts in Northern Nevada this afternoon \ninto this evening. Low forecast confidence in the possibility of \nvalley rain and mountain snow in Northern Nevada this afternoon \nand this evening. Moderate forecast confidence regarding dry and \nrelatively cool conditions Tuesday through Thursday. Moderate \nforecast confidence in increasing precipitation this weekend. No \ndeviations from the NBM model output.",
